    <entry>
        <title type="html"><![CDATA[FF144 cross-origin iframe requires user interaction for top-navigation]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/27960</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/27960"/>
        <updated>2025-09-30T06:39:10.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/5368500?v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><p dir="auto">FF144 adds support for cross-origin <code class="notranslate">&lt;iframe&gt;</code> to require sticky activation and user permission before it can do <code class="notranslate">Window.top</code> navigation (i.e. set <code class="notranslate">top.location</code>) in <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1419501" rel="nofollow">https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1419501</a></p>
<p dir="auto">This was originally supported in Chrome 68 (<a href="https://chromestatus.com/feature/5851021045661696" rel="nofollow">https://chromestatus.com/feature/5851021045661696</a>) and Safari 13 (<a href="https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=193076" rel="nofollow">https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=193076</a>).</p>
<p dir="auto">Note that this is not standard though AFAIK. There are discussions in <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="1272422076" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8013" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/8013/hovercard" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/8013">whatwg/html#8013</a> and all the main browsers comply, but no place to point in the HTML spec. Further there are tests, but these aren't all passed by anyone and are marked as experimental.</p>
<p dir="auto">Note that according to <a href="https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1419501#c28" rel="nofollow">https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1419501#c28</a> if the redirect is blocked then Chrome and FF will prompt for user permission to navigate and log the error in console, while Safari just blocks the navigation and reports and error in the dev console.</p>
<p dir="auto">Related work can be tracked in <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="3417409664" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/41138" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/mdn/content/issues/41138/hovercard" href="https://github.com/mdn/content/issues/41138">mdn/content#41138</a></p>]]></content>
